  :adr/body "## What moved

`cloud.itonami.app.vf-calibrate` (ADR-2609111230 slice 3) is the XMILE
calibration bridge: a measured VF event recalibrates the constants its
resource is bound to, nothing else moves. Most of the file is mechanism the
native word-typed slice deliberately refuses — reading the correspondence
table (a map), `xmodel/lookup`, `Double/parseDouble`, `assoc-in`, assembling
the report maps. All of that stays host.

What moved is the one thing in the file that is a DECISION about plain
booleans: which of the three buckets a correspondence lands in.

    measure absent      -> \"unmatched\"  (the honest default, nothing moves)
    measured + leaf     -> \"rebased\"    (the model's operating point becomes
                                          the journal's measured present)
    measured + not-leaf -> \"ignored\"    (a binding onto a COMPUTED variable)

The cljc's `calibration` routed on this with a `cond` (`(nil? measure)` /
`(leaf-constant? ...)`), and `calibrated-model` applied the same decision
as a rebase test. Both facts that feed the verdict are not on the slice —
whether the inventory carries a measure (a `get-in` on the journal map) and
whether the bound constant is a bare-number equation (`xmodel/lookup` +
`Double/parseDouble`) — so the HOST prepares them as booleans and the core
decides.

Landed this tick:

- `src/cloud/itonami/app/vf_calibrate_core.kotoba` — `calibration-kind`,
  two `:bool` in, `:string` bucket out; a total function of the two
  facts, matching the cljc's `cond` table exactly
- `kotoba_oracle.cljc` `cores` gains `:vf-calibrate-core`
- `vf_calibrate.clj` `calibrated-model` and `calibration` now route on the
  core verdict through `kotoba-oracle`; the doubles (`:from`/`:to`/rebase
  value), the `assoc-in` write, and the report assembly stay host
- parity test `test/cloud/itonami/app/vf_calibrate_kotoba_parity_test.clj`
  + KIR regeneration + JVM-free gen_kir_amu identity

## The split is byte-faithful, not just behavior-preserving

The verdict is a total function of the two booleans, and BOTH cljc functions
already routed on exactly this two-boolean test (the `cond`'s first limb IS
`(nil? measure)`, its second IS the truthiness of `(leaf-constant? ...)`).
So the core expresses the original decision on the native slice; the host
reads the verdict and applies it. Every report row and every rebase is
unchanged: the existing `vf_calibrate_test.clj` (journal append → model
rebase → report rows) now runs through the oracle path and pins it.

Nil discipline (ADR-2609081000): an absent measure is `some?`-false, exactly
like the cljc's `(nil? measure)` limb, and never throws — the core's
absent-measure limb answers \"unmatched\" first.

## Measured bounds on the pinned verifier (amu bin, jvm-free)

- `amu check src/cloud/itonami/app/vf_calibrate_core.kotoba --jvm-free` →
  `:ok true, :effects #{}` (the authoritative gate; measured on the landed
  core)
- nested `if` with `:bool` condition and `:string` literals → admitted
  (the same shape ADR-2609081200 measured for `if`/string returns)
- the doubles, maps, and `Double/parseDouble` do NOT cross the slice — that
  is why `leaf-constant?`, `assoc-in`, and the report maps stay host

## Status

Accepted. The 14-layer topo map (ADR-2609081000) listed `vf-calibrate` as a
layer-0 slice-ready wave-0 candidate; measured 2026-09-08 by
`scripts/kotoba_slice_measure.py` it was slice-ready (no
:import/throw/regex/java/IO), and the landed core passes the JVM-free gate.
Like the human-work-marketplace tick, the candidate was chosen from the
measure by fewest lines after skipping the already-landed
`human_work_marketplace` (also on main as of this landing)."
